","To calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction, we need to find the difference between the entropy of the products and the reactants. The balanced chemical equation for the reaction is:

2 CO (g) + O2 (g) → 2 CO2 (g)

We can use the standard molar entropies (S°) for each substance to calculate the change in entropy. The standard molar entropies for CO, O2, and CO2 are 188.7 J/mol·K, 205.0 J/mol·K, and 213.6 J/mol·K, respectively.

ΔS = ΣnS°(products) - ΣnS°(reactants)

For the given reaction:

ΔS = [2 × 213.6 J/mol·K] - [2 × 188.7 J/mol·K + 1 × 205.0 J/mol·K]

ΔS = (427.2 J/mol·K) - (377.4 J/mol·K + 205.0 J/mol·K)

ΔS = 427.2 J/mol·K - 582.4 J/mol·K

ΔS = -155.2 J/mol·K

Since we have 3 moles of CO, we need to multiply the change in entropy per mole by 3:

ΔS = -155.2 J/mol·K × 3

ΔS = -465.6 J/mol·K

The change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction is -465.6 J/mol·K.
