When solid benzoic acid (H(C₇H₅O₂)) reacts with sodium hydroxide (NaOH), the observable effects are:
1. The solid benzoic acid dissolves in the solution.
2. The solution becomes basic (alkaline) due to the formation of sodium benzoate (Na(C₇H₅O₂)) and water.
3. The solution becomes blue if phenolphthalein indicator is added, indicating the basic nature of the solution.

The fully ionized equation for the reaction is:
\[
\text{H(C₇H₅O₂)} + \text{NaOH} \rightarrow \text{Na(C₇H₅O₂)} + \text{H₂O}
\]
Here, the benzoic acid donates a proton (H⁺) to the hydroxide ion (OH⁻) from sodium hydroxide, forming water and sodium benzoate.

