","Absolute asymmetric synthesis is the enantioselective synthesis of a chiral molecule where the absolute configuration of the product is known and determined. This means that the reaction produces only one enantiomer of the product, and the absolute configuration of that enantiomer is known without the need for comparison with a known reference compound. This is distinct from other types of asymmetric synthesis, where the reaction produces a higher proportion of one enantiomer (enantioselectivity) but the absolute configuration may not be known.

For example, in a case of asymmetric synthesis, the reaction could produce 90% of the (R)-enantiomer of a chiral compound. However, without additional data, the absolute configuration of the (R)-enantiomer would not be known. It could be either the (R)-enantiomer or the (S)-enantiomer, as the (R) and (S) designations are relative to a reference molecule.

In contrast, in an asymmetric synthesis, the absolute configuration of the product is known. This can be achieved by using chiral auxiliaries, chiral catalysts, or enzymatic reactions that selectively produce one enantiomer with a known absolute configuration.

An example of asymmetric synthesis is the synthesis of the chiral amino acid (R)-phenylalanine using the enzyme phenylalanine oxidase. The enzyme catalyzes the conversion of phenylalanine to phenylalanine oxide, and the reaction is highly enantioselective, producing almost exclusively the (R)-enantiomer. The absolute configuration of the (R)-enantiomer is known, as it is the same configuration of the natural (R)-phenylalanine.

In summary, the main difference between asymmetric synthesis and absolute asymmetric synthesis is that in the latter, the absolute configuration of the product is known, while in the first, it may not be known. This distinction is important for the development of chiral drugs and other compounds, as the absolute configuration of a chiral molecule can have a significant impact on its biological activity.
