","The optimal ratio of titanium dioxide (TiO2) nanoparticles and graphene oxide (GO) in a composite material for use in high-performance dye-sensized solar cells (DSSCs) is not a fixed value and depends on several factors, such as the specific synthesis method, the size and shape of the nanoparticles, the concentration of the dye, and the stability of the electrolyte. However, in general, a lower ratio of GO to TiO2 is generally preferred for achieving better performance in DSSCs. This is because GO can act as a charge transfer layer, facilitating the transfer of electrons from the dye to the TiO2 electrode. However, if the ratio of GO to TiO2 is too high, it can lead to a decrease in the overall efficiency of the solar cell due to the increased recombination of electrons and holes.

The optimal ratio of TiO2 and GO can be determined through a series of experiments, such as varying the ratio of the two components and measuring the performance of the resulting DSSCs. Additionally, the stability of the solar cell can be evaluated by measuring the degradation of the solar cell's performance over time under various environmental conditions.

