","The configuration of the chiral alcohol product is determined by the steric environment of the reaction. For both acid chlorides and esters, the Grignard reagent adds to the carbonyl carbon from the less hindered face, leading to the formation of the more stable transition state. This results in the formation of the **R-configured alcohol** when the acid chloride or ester is chiral. 

To experimentally determine the configuration of the resulting chiral alcohol, you can use the following methods:

1. **Polarity--based NMR**: The chemical shift of the hydroxyl proton in the NMR spectrum can be used to determine the configuration of the alcohol. The R-configured alcohol will have a different chemical shift compared to the S-configured alcohol due to the different spatial arrangement of the substituents around the chiral center.

2. **Optical Rotation**: The R- and S-configured alcohols will rotate plane-polarized light in opposite directions. By measuring the optical rotation of the product, you can determine its configuration.

3. **HPLC or HPLC-MS**: The chiral alcohol can be separated using a chiral stationary phase in HPLC. The retention times of the R- and S-configured alcohols will be different, allowing you to distinguish between them. HPLC-MS can be used to confirm the identity of the separated compounds.

4. **X-ray Crystallography**: This is the most definitive method for determining the configuration of a chiral compound. By analyzing the crystal structure of the alcohol, you can determine the spatial arrangement of the atoms and the configuration of the chiral center.

The choice of method will depend on the availability of equipment and the desired level of accuracy.
