","In a double-stranded DNA molecule, the bases pair in a complementary manner: adenine (A) pairs with thymine (T), and guanine (G) pairs with cytosine (C). Therefore, the number of each base in the other strand will be equal to the number of its complementary base in the first strand.

In this case, the double-stranded DNA molecule will have the following number of bases:

A: 20 (from the first strand) + 20 (from the second strand) = 40
G: 25 (from the first strand) + 25 (from the second strand) = 50
C: 30 (from the first strand) + 30 (from the second strand) = 60
T: 22 (from the first strand) + 22 (from the second strand) = 44

So, the complete double-stranded DNA molecule contains 40 adenines, 50 guanines, 60 cytosines, and 44 thyines.
