According to the third law of thermodynamics, perfect crystals at absolute zero have an entropy of zero. What does this law imply about the entropy of imperfect crystals at absolute zero?
</USER_REQUEST>

<GROUND_TRUTH>
The third law of thermodynamics states that a perfect crystal at absolute zero has an entropy of zero. However, if the crystal is imperfect (e.g., due to defects, impurities, or disorder in its structure), it will retain some residual entropy even at absolute zero. This is because imperfections introduce additional microstates that the system can occupy, leading to a non-zero entropy value. Thus, the third law implies that only perfect crystals achieve zero entropy at absolute zero, while imperfect crystals do not. 

The refined answer is boxed as follows:  
$\boxed{\text{Imperfect crystals at absolute zero will have a non-zero entropy due to residual disorder or defects.}}$  

