<start_of_turn>model
","The key difference between σ (sigma) and π (pi) molecular orbitals lies in the electron density distribution:  
- **σ molecular orbital**: The electron density is concentrated along the axis between the nuclei of the bonded atoms. This is a symmetric distribution, meaning the orbital is symmetric about the axis.  
- **π molecular orbital**: The electron density is concentrated above and below the axis between the nuclei of the bonded atoms. This is a non-symmetric distribution, meaning the orbital is antisymmetric about the axis.  

The σ orbital is formed by the head-on overlap of atomic orbitals, while the π orbital is formed by the side-by-side overlap of atomic orbitals.  

The difference in electron density distribution leads to differences in the energy levels and bonding characteristics of the orbitals.  

\boxed{\text{The σ orbital is lower in energy and stronger in bonding than the π orbital.}}
