- **Statement A**: If the sequence \( a_n \) is increasing, then \( S_n \) is also increasing.
  - If \( a_n \) is increasing, each term \( a_n \) is greater than or equal to the previous term \( a_{n-1} \). Thus, the sum \( S_n \) is the sum of all terms up to \( a_n \), which is greater than the sum \( S_{n-1} \) (which includes all terms up to \( a_{n-1} \)). Thus, \( S_n > S_{n-1} \), proving \( S_n \) is increasing.

- **Statement B**: The sequence \( S_n \) is increasing if and only if all terms of the sequence \( a_n \) are positive.
  - This is incorrect because \( S_n \) can be increasing even if \( a_n \) has negative terms. For example, \( a_n = 1 - \frac{1}{n} \) is decreasing but \( S_n \) is increasing.

