   The vapor pressure of water at 20°C is approximately 101.325 kPa.

2. **Gage Pressure Due to Depth**:
   The gage pressure at the inlet pipe is the pressure due to the weight of the water above it.
   \[
   P_{\text{gage}} = \rho g h
   \]
   where:
   - \( \rho = 1000 \, \text{kg/m}^3 \) (density of water at 20°C)
   - \( g = 9.81 \, \text{m/s}^2 \) (acceleration due to gravity)
   - \( h = 5 \, \text{m} \) (depth of the pipe)
   \[
   P_{\text{gage}} = (1000 \, \text{kg/m}^3) \times (9.81 \, \text{m/s}^2) \times (5 \, \text{m}) = 49,050 \, \text{Pa} = 49.05 \, \text{kPa}
   \]

3. **Minimum Inlet Pressure**:
   The minimum inlet pressure required to prevent cavitation is the sum of the gage pressure and the vapor pressure.
   \[
   P_{\text{inlet}} = P_{\text{gage}} + P_{\text{vapor}} = 49.05 \, \text{kPa} + 101.325 \, \text{kPa} = 150.38 \, \text{kPa}
   \]

   Therefore, the minimum inlet pressure required to prevent cavitation is \(\boxed{150.38 \, \text{kPa}}\).
