</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To estimate the contribution of the hard particles to the tensile yield strength \(\sigma_{yr}\) of the alloy, we use the Hall-Ogilings equation:  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = \sigma_{yr0} + \frac{G b^2}{d}
\]  
where:  
- \(\sigma_{yr0}\) is the yield strength of the alloy without the particles,  
- \(G\) is the shear modulus,  
- \(b\) is the Burgers vector,  
- \(d\) is the center-to-center spacing of the particles.  

Given:  
- \(d = 6 \times 10^{-8}\) m  
- \(G = 26 \times 10^9\) N m\(^{-2}\)  
- \(b = 0.286 \times 10^{-9}\) m  

Substituting these values into the equation:  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = \sigma_{yr0} + \frac{(26 \times 10^9)(0.286 \times 10^{-9})^2}{6 \times 10^{-8}}
\]  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = \sigma_{yr0} + \frac{26 \times 10^9 \times 0.286^2 \times 10^{-18}}{6 \times 10^{-8}}
\]  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = \sigma_{yr0} + \frac{26 \times 0.286^2 \times 10^{-10}}{6}
\]  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = \sigma_{yr0} + \frac{26 \times 0.0836 \times 10^{-10}}{6}
\]  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = \sigma_{yr0} + \frac{2.2536 \times 10^{-10}}{6}
\]  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = \sigma_{yr0} + 0.4406 \times 10^{-10} \text{ N m}^{-2}
\]  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = \sigma_{yr0} + 4.406 \times 10^{-11} \text{ N m}^{-2}
\]  

Thus, the contribution of the hard particles to the tensile yield strength is \(\boxed{4.406 \times 10^{-11} \text{ N m}^{-2}}\).

</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

