","The uncertainty principle states that the product of the uncertainty in the energy (\(\Delta E\)) and the uncertainty in time (\(\Delta t\)) is given by:
\[
\Delta E \cdot \Delta t \geq \frac{\hbar}{2}
\]
where \(\hbar = \frac{h}{2\pi}\) is the reduced Planck constant. For \(\hbar = 1.054 \times 10^{-34}\) J·s, the uncertainty in energy is:
\[
\Delta E \geq \frac{\hbar}{2 \Delta t} = \frac{1.054 \times 10^{-34}}{2 \times 2.0 \times 10^{-23}} = 5.27 \times 10^{-12} \text{ J} = 5.27 \times 10^{-12} \text{ J} \times \frac{1 \text{ MeV}}{1.602 \times 10^{-13} \text{ J}} = 34.6 \text{ MeV}
\]

Thus, the typical range of outcomes of measurements of the Σ⁺ rest energy would be:
\[
1385 \text{ MeV} \pm 34.6 \text{ MeV}
\]

or:
\[
\boxed{1350 \text{ MeV} \leq E_{\text{rest}} \leq 1435 \text{ MeV}}
\]
