","To solve this problem, we use the time dilation formula from Einstein's Special Relativity:

\[
t = \frac{t_0}{\sqrt{1 - \frac{v^2}{c^2}}}
\]

Where:
- \( t \) is the time measured by the moving body (the cargo bag clock),
- \( t_0 \) is the time measured by the stationary frame (the cockpit clock),
- \( v \) is the relative velocity of the moving body (0.6c),
- \( c \) is the speed of light.

Given:
- \( t_0 = 3 \) years (elapsed time on the cockpit clock),
- \( v = 0.6c \).

Substitute the values into the formula:

\[
t = \frac{3 \text{ years}}{\sqrt{1 - \left(\frac{0.6c}{c}\right)^2}} = \frac{3 \text{ years}}{\sqrt{1 - 0.36}} = \frac{3 \text{ years}}{\sqrt{0.64}} = \frac{3 \text{ years}}{0.8} = 3.75 \text{ years}
\]

Thus, the clock in the cargo bag will have elapsed \( \boxed{3.75 \text{ years}} \) when the ship returns to Earth.
