","Topological defects are classified based on the symmetry group of the system. In the context of the early universe, the most common types of topological defects are:

1. **Disbles (Disons):** These are one-dimensional defects, such as a line or a string. They can be produced when a phase transition occurs in the early universe, such as the transition from a chiral phase to an non-chiral phase. The defects are formed when the order parameter of the system changes sign along the defect line.

2. **Disons (Disons):** These are two-dimensional defects, such as a ring or a disk. They can be produced when a phase transition occurs in the early universe, such as the transition from a chiral phase to an non-chiral phase. The defects are formed when the order parameter of the system changes sign on the defect area.

3. **Skyons:** These are three-dimensional defects, such as a sphere or a ring. They can be produced when a phase transition occurs in the early universe, such as the transition from a chiral phase to an non-chiral phase. The defects are formed when the order parameter of the system changes sign on the defect volume.

4. **Skyons:** These are three-dimensional defects, such as a sphere or a ring. They can be produced when a phase transition occurs in the early universe, as the order parameter of the system changes sign on the defect volume.

